From: Linux3 Build Project <linux3@example.com>
Date: Thu, 11 Jun 2026 12:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] relocs: Mark per_cpu_load_addr with __attribute__((common))

In GCC 10+, the default changed from -fcommon to -fno-common.
This causes a linker error when building arch/x86/tools/relocs
because per_cpu_load_addr is defined as a non-static global in
relocs.c, which is #include'd by both relocs_32.c and relocs_64.c.

With -fno-common, each tentative definition is treated as a unique
definition, causing a multiple definition error at link time.

Fix by marking per_cpu_load_addr with __attribute__((common)) to
preserve the traditional behavior that the linker merges multiple
tentative definitions.

Signed-off-by: Linux3 Build Project <linux3@example.com>

diff -urN a/arch/x86/tools/relocs.c b/arch/x86/tools/relocs.c
--- a/arch/x86/tools/relocs.c
+++ b/arch/x86/tools/relocs.c
@@ -695,7 +695,7 @@
  *
  */
 static int per_cpu_shndx	= -1;
-Elf_Addr per_cpu_load_addr;
+Elf_Addr per_cpu_load_addr __attribute__((common));
 
 static void percpu_init(void)
 {
